## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of society, the energy crisis and environmental problems have become more and more serious [1]. Therefore, designing of environmentally friendly energy storage conversion devices is one of the important strategies to solve above mentioned problems [2]. Compared with traditional batteries, supercapacitors have the advantages of perfect power density, high security, long cycle life, and wide operating temperature range [3–6]. They are ideal candidates for large-scale energy storage systems and electric vehicle power systems. However, the shortcomings of low specific energy density restrict the use of supercapacitors as energy storage devices. The energy storage and conversion of supercapacitors mainly come from the surface ion absorption/desorption (electric double layer capacitance) and the surface redox reaction (pseudocapacitance) of the electrode material [7]. Therefore, the performance of electrode materials is a key factor to further increase the energy density of supercapacitors [8,9].
Compared with single metal oxides, binary metal oxides are a more potential electrode material due to their more oxidation states, higher conductivity and theoretical specific capacitance [10]. Among which, $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ has received widespread attention because of its environmental friendliness, simple preparation method, and high specific capacity. However, single $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ always has disadvantages such as poor rate capacity, and unsatisfactory cycle life [11]. Therefore, electrode materials with composite structures can take advantage of the synergistic advantages of different structural components to improve the electrochemical performance [12]. First of all, the conductivity of the material can be improved by combining $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ with carbon materials. For example, Mehdi Eskandari et al. compounded $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\$ /carbon nanotubes as electrode material. Its specific capacity can reach $1760\mathrm{F}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the current density was 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ [13]. The honeycomb $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}/$ TRGN-CNTs-S composite structure was prepared by Tong et al. as an electrode material. It had a specific capacity of $1810\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ [14]. The composite electrode material of ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}{\mathsf{G O}}$ synthesized by Zhang et al. had a specific capacity of $3.6\mathsf{F}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at a current density of $5\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ [15]. Secondly, the combination of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ with other transition metal oxides can further increase its specific capacity. For example, Yu et al. designed and synthesized a rod-shaped core–shell structure of Co$\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@Z I F-N i C o_{2}O_{4}},$ and the electrode exhibits a specific capacity of $3.37\mathrm{~F~cm}^{-2}$ at a current density of $4\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ [16]. Zhang et al. synthesized $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}/N i-M O F@C C}$ nanoneedle array structure with a specific area capacitance of $7.23\ \mathrm{F}\ c\mathrm{m}^{-2}$ at a current density of $5\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ [17]. Wang et al. designed and synthesized the $\Nu\mathrm{i}{\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}}/\Nu\mathrm{i}{\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}}\varpi\mathrm{n}$ ano fiber composite structure with nanosheets loaded on the nanoneedles as the electrode material of the supercapacitor. It exhibited a high specific capacitance of $2045.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ when the current density was $1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , which is higher than the individual components of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanosheets and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoneedles [18]. The above studies had proved that the performance of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ composite material is better than that of its single composition.
ZIF-67 is a zeolite imidazole ester framework material with cobalt as the metal ligand. It not only has the high stability of inorganic zeolite, but also the advantage of high porosity of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) [19,20]. Therefore, ZIF-67 is widely used as a sacrificial template to synthesize cobalt based oxides in energy storage materials [21]. These oxides can retain the structural advantages of MOFs, which is conducive to ion transport and diffusion, and provides more redox reaction active sites to obtain higher specific capacity [22–24]. Such as Qian et al. used the in-situ growth method to grow the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ nanocomposite material derived from the MOFs on the nickel foam. The in-situ growth method could ensure a firm composite structure without additional additives, and reduce the impedance of the electrode material. Moreover, this method could ensure the orderly growth of the array structures and avoid the agglomeration of the composite structures [25]. To the best of our knowledge, MOFs derived multi-dimensional $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ composites combined with self-supporting carbon substrate has not been reported.
In this study, we prepared multi-dimensional $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ hierarchical nanosheets on graphene/carbon sphere $\scriptstyle\left(\operatorname{rGO}/\operatorname{CS}\right)$ conductive substrate film through an in-situ MOF derivatization method. The material can synergize the characteristics of carbon substrate and multi-element transition metal oxides to achieve complementary advantages, improving the overall conductivity and specific capacitance of the electrode. It shows an ultra-high specific capacity of $3876.6\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(538.4~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ ) when tested at a current density of $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . A hybrid supercapacitor assembled by commercial activated carbon (AC) and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/}$ CS, the energy density at a power density of $800~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1}$ is calculated to be 56.5 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ . After 6000 charge–discharge cycles, $92.5\%$ of the initial capacity can be maintained, which proves the excellent electrochemical properties of the supercapacitor.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
All the chemicals used were analytical grade without purification. $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , graphite powder, glucose, $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ , HCl, $\mathsf{K M n O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and urea were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. 2-Methylimidazole was purchased from Aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd.
## 2.2. Synthesis
2.2.1. Synthesis of the $N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S$ composite film
The modified Hummer method was used to synthesize graphene oxide (GO) [26]. The CS were prepared according to Liu’s [27] method. Firstly, $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of CS and $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of GO $(0.2\mathrm{\mg/mL})$ 1 suspension were mixed uniformly and vacuum filtered to a film. Secondly, $0.559\textrm{g C o}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $0.279\textrm{g N i}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $_{0.291\mathrm{~g~}}$ urea and $0.059\mathrm{~g~NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ were added in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water to be fully dissolved by ultrasound. Thirdly, the mixed solution in the step two and the prepared flexible film were mixed together and reacted at $110^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . After the reactor was cooled to room temperature, the samples were washed alternately with deionized water and ethanol for several times. Finally, the obtained $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{O}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/\mathrm{CS}$ composite film was dried at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
2.2.2. Synthesis of the $C o_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S$ composite film
The as-prepared ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ composite film was firstly put into $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of 2-methylimidazole solution $\langle32.5\mathrm{\mg/mL}\rangle$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ to synthesize ZIF-67 $I_{\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}}@\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{CS}$ film. After the reaction, the composite film is washed by deionized water and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Finally, the ZIF-67/ $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/\mathsf{C S}$ composite film was calcined in a vacuum at $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a temperature rate of $5^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to get the final sample $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ composite film. $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ powder without substrate was also prepared for comparison.
## 2.3. Materials characterization
The field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM) (JSM7001F/Japan jeol) was used to characterize the surface morphology of the sample. The transmission electron microscope (TEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM) (JEM2100F (HR)/Japan jeol) were used to detect the internal structure of the sample and the elemental composition of the sample. Xray diffractometer (XRD, XRD-6100 Shimadzu Instruments Co., Ltd) with Cu Pd $\mathfrak{a}$ radiation was used to characterize the composition of the sample. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo ESCALAB 250XI Thermo Fisher Scientific) was used to detect the valence elements. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) is used to test the specific surface area using a Quantachrome Autosorb-1 apparatus.
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical tests of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ selfsupporting electrode were carried out on the basis of CHI 760E (Shanghai Chenhua) electrochemical workstation. Firstly, the film electrode in this experiment is directly used as the positive material (without any additives). $\mathrm{\sfHg/HgO}$ was used as the reference electrode and Pt was used as the counter electrode. 6 M KOH was used as the electrolyte for the cyclic voltammetry (CV) test, galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) test and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) test. In the two-electrode system, commercial AC, acetylene black and polytetrafluoroethylene were mixed with a mass ratio of 8:1:1 on a piece of Ni foam ( $1.0\ \mathrm{cm}$ $\times~1.0~\mathrm{cm})$ as the negative electrode. The CV and GCD of twoelectrodes were tested by assembling $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ and AC electrodes to form a hybrid supercapacitor.
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 describes the preparation process of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@-}$ $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{rGO}}/{\mathrm{CS}}$ composite film. Firstly, GO and CS dispersion were mixed together and vacuum filtered to form a $\mathsf{G O}/\mathsf{C S}$ film. Secondly, $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ with nanoneedle and flake structure were grown on ${\bf G}{\bf O}{\bf\Lambda}$ CS substrate by a hydrothermal method. Thirdly, ZIF-67 nanoparticles were generated on $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanosheets by an in-situ growth method. Finally, the sample was annealed at $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to obtain the final self-supporting electrode material of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}.$
Fig. 2 is the SEM and TEM characterization of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}}.$ $\mathrm{O}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/\mathrm{CS}$ composite film. Fig. 2a and $\mathsf{d}$ are the top view SEM images of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ on $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{rGO}}/{\mathrm{CS}}$ substrate. We can observe the synthesized $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ uniformly loaded on the rGO/CS film with a nano-needle-like structure coating on flakes, and these flakes automatically combines into a thorny flower. These flakes have a thickness of about $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Fig. 2b and e show the formation of ZIF-67 particles on the edge of flakes by soaking ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ in dimethylimidazole. From the inset picture in Fig. 2b, it can be seen that the color of the composite film has changed to purple. Fig. $2\mathsf{c}$ and f are the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}@\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{CS}$ samples after calcining. From the figures, we can observe the flakes are thinner and the particles are obviously shriveled. The multi-dimensional $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ hierarchical flakes on self-supporting carbon substrate provides a wider space for the contact of electrolyte. Besides, the inset in
Fig. 3c shows that the color of the sample finally turned black after calcination. Fig. S1a is a cross-sectional SEM image of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}}$ - $\begin{array}{r}{0_{4}@\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{CS}.}\end{array}$ . It can be seen that the flakes of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ are distributed on the surface of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{rGO}}/{\mathrm{CS}}$ composite conductive substrate. The cross-section thickness of the rGO/CS film is around $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and the thickness of the composite film is about $12.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Fig. S1b is a partial enlarged view of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{rGO}}/{\mathrm{CS}}$ substrate. We can see that the composite film is a sandwich structure with CS interspersed between rGO nanosheets. This structure is beneficial to the transmission of electrolyte between graphene nanosheets. Fig. $2\mathrm{g}$ is the TEM image of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ composite. It can be seen that the sample structure is composed of some polyhedrons loaded on a thin sheet with needles on the edge, which is consistent with the SEM results. Fig. 2h is a partial enlarged view of Fig. 2g, the nanoneedles of the material structure are staggered on the edge of the sheet. It can be measured that the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ polyhedron is about $465~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and the thickness of the nanoneedles on the sheet is $30\sim40~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Fig. 2i is the HRTEM image of the composites. The interplanar distances of $0.245\mathrm{nm}$ , $0.244\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.203\mathrm{nm}$ match well with the (311), (311) and (400) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ [28,29]. Through the element mapping (Fig. 2j-l) in Fig. 2h, it can be seen that the elements are uniformly distributed, and there are only Co and O on the loaded polyhedron, which shows that the composition is cobalt oxide. The surface area of the composite is also tested and it is $168.6\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , in addition, the average pore diameter is found nearly $2.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ by the poresize distribution analysis (Fig. S2). Such a large specific surface area can provide more active sites for electrochemical reaction, appropriate pore size distribution is also conducive to the transmission of electrolyte.
Fig. 3a shows the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}@\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{CS}$ and ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ It is found that the peaks of ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ at $31.15^{\circ}$ (2 2 0), $36.7^{\circ}$ (3 1 1), $44.6^{\circ}$ (400) and $64.98^{\circ}$ (440), which are consistent with the characteristic peaks of the $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ standard spectrum (JCPDS No.20–0781) [30]. Therefore, it is proved that $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ was successfully prepared [31,32]. However, the characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ in the pattern of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}\textcircled{2}-$ rGO/CS were weakened. This may be due to the formation of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ covers on the surface of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ so that the characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ cannot be revealed. Furthermore, there are several characteristic peaks at $31.27^{\circ}$ (2 2 0), $36.85^{\circ}$ (3 1 1), $44.81^{\circ}$ (4 0 0), $59.35^{\circ}$ (511) and $65.23^{\circ}$ (440) [33], which corresponds to the characteristic peak of the standard $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS No.43–1003). It indicates that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ has been generated [23]. In order to more accurately determine the composition and element valence of the composite material, we further performed XPS test analysis. Fig. 3b is the full spectrum of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ composites. It is found that the material mainly contains four elements: nickel, cobalt, carbon, and oxygen. Fig. 3c shows the spectrum and fitting curves of ${\mathsf{N i}}2{\mathsf{p}}$ . Four obvious peaks can be seen in the spectrum. The peaks at $855.6\mathrm{eV}$ and $873.8\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ spin–orbit peaks respectively. The peaks at $861.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $880.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to the satellite peaks which are recorded as ‘‘sat”. [31]. Fig. 3d is a high-definition spectrum of O1s, in which three peaks at $529.8\mathrm{~eV}$ , $531.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $533.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ represent the $\mathsf{N i}{-}0$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C o}}-0$ and $0{-}\mathsf{C}{=}0$ peaks, respectively [34,35]. Fig. 3e is the C1s spectrum, in which the three fitted peaks represent the $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ peak at $284.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ the $c{-}0$ peak at $285.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ and the $\scriptstyle0-C=0$ peak at 288.9 eV. Fig. 3f is a high-definition spectrum of $\mathtt{C o}\ 2\mathtt{p}$ . $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at $780.6\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ spin–orbit peaks at 796.1 eV are observed [32]. The XPS test and analysis data can further confirm the synthesis of ${C o_{3}}{0_{4}}/$ ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ composites.
Fig. 4 shows the electrochemical performance test and analysis of the prepared sample and its comparative samples. Fig. 4a is the CV comparison curves of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ powder, ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ film and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ film electrodes at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . It is found that the cycle diagram has a clear redox peak, which indicates that the redox reaction has occurred [36]. Therefore, the capacitance characteristics exhibited by the material are pseudo-capacitance characteristics. Furthermore, the circulation area of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}@\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{CS}$ is significantly larger than that of ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ . Since the capacity is proportional to its circulation area, the specific capacity of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}}.$ $\mathrm{O}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/\mathrm{CS}$ is greater than that of ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ and ${C o_{3}O_{4}}/$ $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ . Fig. 4b is the charge and discharge comparison curve of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ , ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . Firstly, judging from the shape of the charge and discharge, there is an obvious platform in the figure, which is also consistent with CV results. Therefore, the capacitance characteristics exhibited by the electrode materials belong to pseudo-capacitance characteristics. Secondly, it is found that the discharge time of ${C o_{3}}{0_{4}}/$ ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ is longer than that of ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\Gamma}\mathsf{G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ , so the specific capacity of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ is larger than that of $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ . Fig. 4c is a graph that visually reflects the capacity of the three materials at different current densities. The specific capacity is calculated based on the GCD test data according to formula (1) [37,38]:
$$
C=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{\mathbf{m}\times\Delta V}
$$
where $c$ represents the specific capacitance/F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , $I$ represents the test current/A, $\Delta t$ represents the discharge time/s, m represents the mass of the active material $/\mathrm{g}$ and $\Delta V$ represents the test voltage window/V. The calculated specific capacitance of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@\bar{\omega}_{-}}$ - rGO/CS are 3876.6 F ${\bf g}^{-1}$ (538.4 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ), 3561.2F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $(494.6\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.\$ , $3421.8\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(475.25\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1})$ , $3284\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-}~\$ 1 $(456.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1};$ ), $3163.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(439.3\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}},$ and $3092\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(429.4~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}};$ at $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $3\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , $5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $8\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ and $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. These values are much higher than another two electrodes of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ and ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{r}{\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{O}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{S}}$ . Fig. 4d is the CV curves of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ in a voltage window of $_{0-0.7\mathrm{V}}$ at a scan rate of $2{-}100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . It is found that the CV curve at each rate has a pair of symmetric redox peaks, indicating that the redox reaction occurred at the corresponding position. The reaction process is shown below [10,39].
$$
\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}\ +\ H_{2}O\ +\ O H^{-}{\longleftrightarrow}N i O O H\ +\ 2C o O O H\ +\ e^{-}(2)}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}\ +\ O H^{-+}H_{2}O{\leftarrow}{\rightarrow}}3\mathrm{CoOOH}\ +\ e^{-}(3)
$$
$$
\mathrm{CoOOH}+\mathrm{OH^{-}}{\longleftrightarrow}\mathrm{CoO_{2}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+e^{-}(4)
$$
Fig. 4e is the charge–discharge test curves of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@.}$ $\operatorname{rGO}/\operatorname{CS}$ under different current densities. It is found that the charging and discharging shapes are consistent at different current densities, and the specific capacity can still be maintained at $79.8\%$ when the current density is 10 times. The good rate properties of the electrode are demonstrated. In addition, the significant voltage platform also proved that the electrode material is a battery-type electrode. We further tested the cycling performance of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ in a three-electrode configuration. As shown in Fig. S4, $93.9\%$ capacitance still maintained after 3000 cycles, indicating the good stability of the electrode. EIS tests were also performed on the three electrode materials, as shown in Fig. 4f. According to the principle that the impedance in the high frequency area is characterized by resistance, it is found that the equivalent series resistance of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ is smaller than that of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ and ${\mathsf{N i C o}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{r}{\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{O}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{S}}$ . The EIS curve is generally composed of a semicircle in the high frequency area and a straight line in the low frequency area [40]. Only ${C o_{3}}{0_{4}}/$ $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ has a small semicircle caused by contact resistance, which shows that the composite film has higher conductivity [41]. In addition, the hierarchical structure of the composite film makes it easy to transfer charges in the electrolyte. Therefore, the contact resistance is very small [5]. In the low frequency region, the linear slope of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ electrode material is greater than that of the other two electrode materials, indicating that the structure of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ is more conducive to the diffusion of electrolyte. The self-supporting composite electrode material designed in this paper has good electrochemical properties may be due to the following reasons: Firstly, the carbon spheres are evenly dispersed between the graphene sheets to avoid stacking of graphene and improve the utilization of graphene. Besides, the rGO/CS film can play its conductive advantage in the whole composite material and improve the electron transfer rate [42]. Secondly, the synergy between Ni and Co bimetallic oxides can provide more active sites for redox reactions. Thirdly, this multi-dimensional morphology structure can increase the effective contact area between the composite material and the electrolyte, further accelerating the migration of ions.
To further explore the pseudocapacitance behavior of electrode materials, the charge storage mechanism of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/}$ CS electrodes was studied and analyzed. A CV test at a scan rate of $1.5{-}3.5\mathrm{~mV~}s^{-1}$ was done, as shown in Fig. 5a. By analyzing the scan rates and the peak current, the electrochemical behavior can be distinguished whether it is a diffusion behavior or a pseudo-capacitance behavior during the charging and discharging process. For electrode materials, the value of $b$ can be calculated by formula (5) to determine whether it is pseudocapacitance behavior during charging and discharging [43,44]:
$$
i=a\nu^{b}
$$
When $b<0.5$ , the electrode material exhibits battery properties; when $0.5<b<1$ , the electrode material exhibits battery properties and pseudocapacitance properties; when $b>1$ , the electrode material exhibits pseudocapacitance properties [45]. From Fig. 5a we can get the peak current values at different voltage sweep rates [46]. Take the logarithm of both sides of formula (5) to get formula (6) [47]:
$$
\mathsf{l o g}i=b\mathsf{l o g}\upsilon+\mathsf{l o g}a
$$
According to the voltage sweep rate and the corresponding peak current, the value of $b$ (slope) can be obtained by linear fitting log i and log $\nu$ in formula (6) through the origin mathematical software. Fig. 5b shows the value of $b$ under different peak currents. From the analysis results, it can be seen that the $b$ during the charging and discharging process is 0.6337 and 0.6276, respectively. It indicates that the performance of battery and pseudocapacity coexist when the electrode is working [48]. Formula (7) can be used to calculate the pseudo-capacitance contribution rate:
$$
i(V)/\nu^{1/2}=\mathtt{h}_{1}\nu^{1/2}+\mathtt{h}_{2}
$$
In order to verify the application potential of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}}.$ $\mathrm{O}_{4}@\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/\mathrm{CS}$ composite electrode material, as shown in Fig. 6a, a hybrid supercapacitor was assembled. The $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/}$ CS composite electrode material is used as the positive electrode and commercial activated carbon is used as the negative electrode. Fig. 6b is the CV curves of AC and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S}$ composite electrode materials measured at a scan rate of $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ in a three-electrode system. The CV curve of commercial activated carbon does not show a redox peak, indicating that it is a typical electric double layer capacitance material. In order to find the appropriate voltage window, a CV test with different voltage windows was done from $_{0-1\mathrm{~V~}}$ to $_{0-1.8\mathrm{~V~}}$ , As shown in Fig. 6c. From the test results, the curve polarization occurs when the voltage window over $1.6\:\mathrm{V}$ Therefore, it is finally determined that the test voltage window is $_{0-1.6\mathrm{V}.}$ Fig. 6d is a series of CV curves of ${C o_{3}O_{4}}/$ $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathsf{r G O}/{\mathsf{C S}}//\mathsf{A C}$ devices at different scanning rates. The CV curve presents a rectangular shape without significant redox peaks, which indicates that the electrodes measured under the two-electrode system exhibit electric double layer capacitance behavior and Faraday pseudocapacitance behavior. In addition, the shape of the CV curves remains consistent with the scanning rate increases, which shows the ideal fast charge and discharge characteristics of the supercapacitor. Fig. 6e is the GCD curves diagram of the device with different current densities under a voltage window of $0{-}1.6\ \mathrm{V}.$ It can be seen from the test results that the curve has good symmetry, indicating that the device has good electrochemical performance. Fig. 6f is the capacity diagram of the device calculated based on GCD data, where the specific capacities at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , $2\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , $3\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , $5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , $8\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ and ${10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}}$ are $159.0\mathrm{F}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , $133.3\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , $120.9\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $106.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , $89.0\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $79.4\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively, which shows that the device has good specific capacity and rate properties.
Cycle stability is one of the most important performance evaluation standards of supercapacitor. As shown in Fig. 7a, we test 6000 charge–discharge cycles for the device, the results show that $92.5\%$ capacitance retention, indicating its superior stability. It can also be observed that there is a rising trend of capacitance from around 1000 circles, which may be due to the activation of the electrode material during the test. Therefore, the material has good application prospects as electrode materials. The insets in the Fig. 7a are the GCD curves of the first 10 cycles and the last 10 cycles, the discharge time is slightly shortened. Fig. 7b is the energy density and power density diagram. The results show that the energy density can reach up to 56.5 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at the power density of $800~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ . The energy density of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}@r G O/C S//}$ AC supercapacitor device is higher than that of some capacitors with similar composition. For example $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}/r G O//A C}$ C (57 Wh $\displaystyle{\log^{-1}}$ at 375 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [49], $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}/r G O/C F//A C}$ (24.6 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $8477.7~\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1};$ [50], $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}/$ TRGN-CNTs-S//NGN/CNTs (54 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at 799.9 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [14], $\mathrm{CoCo_{3}O_{4}@Z I F-N i C o_{2}O_{4}}//\mathrm{AC}$ (50.6 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $856~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}.$ ) [16], GO@NiCoP//AG (27.71 Wh $\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at 788 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [47], $\mathrm{CNT}@\mathrm{NiCo-LDH}//\mathrm{CNT}@\mathrm{NC}$ (37.4 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $750~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}};$ [51], $\Nu\mathrm{i}\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\Nu\mathrm{-rGO}//\mathsf{A C}$ (20.1 Wh $\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at 720.71 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [52], $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}/N i_{2}P//A C}$ (40.7 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $800~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}\mathrm{.}$ [53]. In order to explore the practical value of the supercapacitor, we also connected 25 LED bulbs in series for lighting experiments, as shown in the illustration in Fig. 7b. The successful lighting of the LEDs proves that the device has a certain practical potential.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully synthesized multidimensional $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ hierarchical nanosheets on selfsupporting $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{rGO}}/{\mathrm{CS}}$ substrate electrodes. The electrochemical test results show that the electrode material exhibits ultra-high capacitance, which is much higher than the capacity of carbon-based electrode materials reported previously. In the unique structure, graphene/carbon sphere substrate can significantly improve the conductivity of the whole electrode. Moreover, multidimensional $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ hierarchical flakes can enhance the contact area with the electrolyte, which is conducive to ion migration and efficient Faraday reaction to increase the specific capacity. This will provide new ideas for the design and synthesis of composite materials of redox graphene and transition metal oxides. When assembled a hybrid supercapacitor, the device shows a high energy density and an ideal capacitance retention rate, proving that the material has potential for future applications. Our synthesis method is expected to provide some ideas for the development of high capacitance self-supporting electrode materials.
